<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Valentinian I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">321–375</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Valentinian the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Valentinian I was Roman emperor from 364 to 375. A Pannonian officer acclaimed by the army after Jovian's death, he gave the East to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> and ruled the West himself, spending most of his reign fortifying the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> frontiers against the Alemanni, Quadi and Sarmatians. He died of a stroke at Brigetio in 375 while angrily addressing envoys of the Quadi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valentinian-i.jpg" alt="Valentinian I" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ANS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_Solidus_of_Valens,_Antioch.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Valentinian was born at Cibalae in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> into a family of Illyro-Roman origin. He and his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> were the sons of Gratianus, nicknamed Funarius, a military officer noted for his wrestling skills, who served as comes Africae and later comes Britanniarum. Valentinian joined the army in the late 330s. Later he served as a tribune under the Caesar Julian in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, but after a failed attempt to intercept Alemannic raiders he and a colleague were blamed and cashiered. He then lived on the family estate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a>, and the sources disagree about whether he left Julian's service over pagan sacrifices or was simply dismissed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After Jovian's death the officials meeting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> rejected other candidates and settled on Valentinian as a man well qualified and nearby. He accepted the acclamation on 25 or 26 February 364 and, to forestall a succession crisis, named his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> co-Augustus at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> on 28 March. Valens took the East, while Valentinian held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and Illyricum, with his court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>.</p><p>In 365 the Alemanni crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, and Valentinian stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> rather than march east. In 368 he crossed the Rhine and the Main with his son Gratian and fought the Alemanni at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-solicinium">Battle of Solicinium</a>, a Roman victory with heavy losses. He built and refurbished fortifications along the Rhine's west bank. In 367 the Great Conspiracy struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and he sent Theodosius, who restored order there, and later put down the revolt of Firmus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. In 373 the Roman commander Marcellianus murdered the Quadic king Gabinius at a banquet, which brought the Quadi and Sarmatians into war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Valentinian crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> into Quadi territory in 374 and wintered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savaria">Savaria</a>. He reached Brigetio on 17 November 375 and received a Quadi deputation, who insisted that the Roman forts built in their lands had caused the conflict. Enraged, he shouted abuse at the envoys and suffered a fatal stroke. His sons Gratian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-ii">Valentinian II</a> succeeded him in the West. Ammianus calls him violent, hostile to the educated and reportedly cruel to servants, yet also an able painter and sculptor. Modern historians such as Michael Grant nonetheless rate him a superb soldier and conscientious worker who tolerated differences of religious opinion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Valentinian I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Valentinian I</b> fought them again after the king Rando sacked Mainz in 368, winning a hard battle at Solicinium, and in 378 Gratian defeated the Lentienses near Argentaria in Alsace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Emperor <b>Valentinian I</b>, who ruled the West while his brother Valens held the East, named his eldest son Gratian, aged eight, co-Augustus.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-24">24 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At Constantinople, the Roman emperor <b>Valentinian I</b> made his brother Valens co-Augustus, giving him the eastern half of the empire and keeping the West for himself.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-28">28 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 374 the killing of their king Gabinius by a Roman officer provoked a raid into Pannonia, and Emperor <b>Valentinian I</b> died in 375 while confronting their envoys.</blockquote><a href="/state/quadi">Quadi</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Vinkovci, in what is now <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>. Died in Brigetio, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.28,18.8&amp;year=356" aria-label="Vinkovci on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Valentinian I"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4330 6188h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4330 6188h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.28,18.8&amp;year=356">What was here in 356 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
